<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><span class="status-pill">In progress</span><div class="eyebrow">Frontend · React · TypeScript · Vite</div><h1>Reel Good — Movie Discovery Web Application</h1><p class="section-lead">A user-facing movie discovery application being developed as part of the FlyRank frontend track.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="container content">
<div class="plain-grid"><div><h2>In plain English</h2><p>A web application that lets people browse movies, search for titles, open detailed movie pages and save favourites.</p></div><div><h2>Why it matters</h2><p>It demonstrates the user-facing side of product development: taking data from an external service and turning it into a usable, accessible interface.</p></div><div><h2>How it works</h2><p>React/TypeScript interface → live OMDb search and detail enrichment → routing and UI states → Firebase authentication and user-scoped favourites architecture.</p></div></div>
<h2>Current implementation</h2>
<ul>
<li>React + TypeScript + Vite application architecture.</li>
<li>Live OMDb title search with pagination, loading, empty and error states, request cancellation and stale-response protection.</li>
<li>Movie detail pages, genre browsing, poster fallbacks and verified IMDb links.</li>
<li>Server-side OMDb proxy with caching, request limiting and bounded detail enrichment.</li>
<li>Firebase email/password authentication and UID-scoped favourites architecture.</li>
<li>Vitest and React Testing Library coverage for routing, live movie flows and authentication/favourites behaviour.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Product context</h2>
<p>This project broadens my portfolio beyond modelling and backend systems by showing how I structure a user-facing application, integrate an external API, handle asynchronous UI states and design personal account features.</p>
<div class="progress-note"><strong>Status:</strong> the broader frontend capstone is still in progress. The movie application is publicly previewable, but Firebase/live end-to-end verification and later frontend-capstone work are still being completed. It is therefore intentionally not presented as a finished capstone.</div>
